  ],
  "text": "Kilogram for kilogram, bone carries more load than steel. And steel cannot heal. Anything hard enough to resist crushing is usually brittle enough to shatter. Bone gets around that by being two materials at the same time. Collagen fibres give it flex, so it bends before it snaps. Mineral crystals packed around those fibres give it hardness. And the shaft is hollow, which is how you get the strength without the mass. The strange part is that it is not finished. It is being rebuilt right now. One set of cells dissolves old bone. Another lays fresh bone down behind them. So the skeleton follows the load. Astronauts lose it in orbit because nothing is pulling. Carry something heavy and your bones thicken to meet it. Steel just wears out. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
